#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bd2d24-7148-5ea0-938f-3c79b0f3f1dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7c99605a-2d55-5d37-b363-2e33fa02575c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10456" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y} & =1-x \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =3(1-x) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(3(1-x))}^{2} \\\\ y & =9(1-x)^{2} \\\\ y & =9(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = 9(x - 1)^2.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f(x) \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\leq 1, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192403 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65cc2d11-c785-538a-a64d-5d3349040676" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Radical Function Containing a Linear Argument"}] :question/id #uuid "8f01f2b7-13d5-58d3-8e2e-9f6804993094" :question/math-academy-id "e-10457" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4x + 1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4x + 1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4y + 1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4y+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{4y+1} & =5x \\\\ {(\\sqrt{4y+1})}^{2} & ={(5x)}^{2} \\\\ 4y+1 & =25{x}^{2} \\\\ 4y & =25{x}^{2}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}(25{x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\left(25x^2 - 1\\right).\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx \\geq -\\dfrac14, \\qquad f(x) \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq -\\dfrac14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192404 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10be7535-989f-5706-964e-06c02251d1f5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Unknown Value Given the Inverse of a Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "8760afc0-a7fb-5398-a585-c5559c9d1bb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10458" :question/problem "The function $f(x) = 3\\sqrt{x + k} - 2$ has an inverse function given by $f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x + 2)^2 + 3.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\sqrt{x+k} - 2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = 3\\sqrt{y + k} - 2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{y+k}-2 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{y+k} & =x+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{y+k} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+2) \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y+k})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}(x+2))}^{2} \\\\ y+k & =\\frac{1}{9}(x+2)^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{9}(x+2)^{2}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x + 2)^2 - k.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-k = 3 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad k = -3.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx \\geq 3, \\qquad f(x) \\geq -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq -2, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192415 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe915c05-2906-5873-8fd5-f364816f83bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Reciprocal Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f689c3e3-9bb9-5e1e-b69a-b691373fdee0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10496" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function ![](images/b9/b9a5eece426fa2aa66aac43b9afaf2812dcf483795a84d5e96fa89c0dcd71db6.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To find ![](images/6e/6e9ea2da83f324dd20158e4b08fb1c6968b6387d458964f21ed03648be954752.svg) we start by replacing ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) with ![](images/02/028bcadf67e469d96df97e89362f88bb8edca3ddea5579906987cc124663fba8.svg)\n![](images/d4/d4f5098eaa3eb85b97ec9547373f138922d22d734018ede816930c665248c0d5.svg)\n\nNow, interchanging ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) gives\n![](images/35/35c51b7dff42eeb3c3b2033c78495c07294048ba02628e583382c0807e43d192.svg)\n\nThen, we make ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) the subject of the equation, as follows:\n![](images/18/183e560a55c159a77b1ceacec766572e2279ad4e552242d638a9fdcf96f2c859.svg)\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n![](images/b5/b55f51d46d8917e5a44e4dae697c71a004580924cecd5a5f079e5eac7f480b3a.svg)\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) are\n![](images/f2/f2f23ba499fcbb1dee1964f08453be99bdf5ed7ec11581b773845c4aa307b64a.svg)\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ![](images/90/90bf8beda1404c3f6e9d9d9565922945fb59df9b0488050e3d2b72a153addd3c.svg) are\n![](images/96/96d9c5432e8f14ac6d9bc1841e37772cad060e3d09065129f73161726aeeffd2.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65cc2d11-c785-538a-a64d-5d3349040676" :knowled